ed 82% of the time&amp;#10;Based on 85 days&amp;#10;outside the perfect-forecast range - real miscalibration\"><circle class=\"chart-dot sig\" cx=\"410.2508235294118\" cy=\"82.23529411764707\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"235.0\" y=\"422\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What the forecast promised</text><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"15\" y=\"200.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 15 200.0)\">How often it actually rained</text></svg>\n<figcaption>Green band = where a <b>flawless</b> forecast would still land, given\nhow few days we have. Red dots fall outside it, so those are real errors rather\nthan noise. The faint horizontal line is climatology\n(56%).</figcaption></figure>\n","season":"\n<h2 id=\"season\">Does the season matter?</h2>\n<p>In Saint John&#x27;s the forecast is most skilful in\n<b>spring</b> (0.16) and least in\n<b>winter</b> (-0.00).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th class=\"num\">Days</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Rain days</th><th class=\"num\">Skill score</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Avg error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>winter</td><td class='num'>170</td><td class='num'>52%</td><td class='num'>-0.003</td><td class='num'>14.9%</td></tr><tr><td>summer</td><td class='num'>174</td><td class='num'>56%</td><td class='num'>0.113</td><td class='num'>11.5%</td></tr><tr><td>autumn</td><td class='num'>175</td><td class='num'>59%</td><td class='num'>0.152</td><td class='num'>8.8%</td></tr><tr><td>spring</td><td class='num'>152</td><td class='num'>56%</td><td class='num'>0.156</td><td class='num'>8.0%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"muted\">Four-way splits of a two-year record are thin, so read the\nordering as indicative. Coarser binning is used here than for the annual curve\nfor the same reason.</p>\n"},"lat":17.12096,"lon":-61.84329,"n":671,"n_cities":213,"name":"Saint John's","offset_days":0,"pop_map":[{"hi":0.14,"lo":0.0,"mean":0